9. I would rename target depending on your philosophy

target is actually a good generic name here.

The UI layer doesn't know whether it's:

current → hovered
current → selected
current → resume
current → focus
current → preferred child

All it knows is:

source + purpose → target

That's arguably the fundamental abstraction:

    For this context and this user-defined purpose, associate another context.

That's more general than "navigation".

You could even call the structure:

UiRelation

instead of UiNavigation.

But if the feature is primarily intended for navigation, UiNavigation is perfectly reasonable.
10. One thing I would change in your API order

You proposed:

ui_layer_nav_set(
    &UI_STATE_A,
    curr_id,
    target_id,
    Hover
);

I think that's good.

I'd perhaps make the signature:

bool ui_layer_nav_set(
    UiState *state,
    ContextId source,
    ContextId target,
    UiPurpose purpose
);

The names source and target make the semantics obvious:

(source, purpose) → target

Then:

ui_layer_nav_set(
    &UI_STATE_A,
    current,
    hovered,
    UI_PURPOSE_HOVERED
);

reads nicely.
11. And yes, UiState stays wonderfully generic

It becomes:

typedef struct UiState {
    ContextId current;

    UiSelection selection;

    UiNavigation navigation;
} UiState;

That's it.

It doesn't know about:

hover
resume
focus
back
forward
preferred

The application does.

For example, one application might define:

enum {
    NAV_HOVERED = 1,
    NAV_RESUME  = 2,
    NAV_FOCUS   = 3,
};

Another might define:

enum {
    NAV_MOUSE = 1,
    NAV_KEYBOARD = 2,
    NAV_SEARCH = 3,
    NAV_LAST_OPENED = 4,
};

The UI library doesn't care.
12. And multiple UiStates become very powerful

You can have:

UiState explorer;
UiState sidebar;
UiState search;

all pointing into the same context layer.

Then:

explorer.navigation:
    (A, HOVERED) → B
    (A, RESUME)  → C

sidebar.navigation:
    (A, HOVERED) → D
    (A, RESUME)  → E

search.navigation:
    (A, FOCUS)   → F

No conflict.

The contexts remain completely unaware of any of this.
13. One concern: your wording "unique key from curr_id and Hover"

You don't necessarily need to physically create a combined key.

For example, don't necessarily do:

uint64_t key = ((uint64_t)context << 32) | purpose;

unless your ID/purpose sizes make that safe and convenient.

I'd simply implement the hash over both:

uint64_t hash_navigation_key(
    ContextId context,
    UiPurpose purpose
);

For example conceptually:

hash = hash_context_id(context);
hash = hash_combine(hash, purpose);

Then equality is:

entry->context == context &&
entry->purpose == purpose

This avoids unnecessary packing assumptions about ContextId.
14. And the growth behavior fits perfectly

Your table can use:

initial capacity: 16
load factor:      ~0.7
growth:           ×2

So:

16 entries
    ↓
11 entries → grow
    ↓
32
    ↓
22 entries → grow
    ↓
64

All entries remain in one contiguous allocation:

malloc:
┌──────┬──────┬──────┬──────┬──────┬──────┐
│ entry│ entry│ entry│ entry│ entry│ ...  │
└──────┴──────┴──────┴──────┴──────┴──────┘

No allocation per relationship.

That's particularly nice because a UI state might have:

50,000 contexts
but only 150 navigation relationships

and you allocate only for those ~150 relationships.
15. I would add remove and perhaps clear

The core API I'd settle on is:

bool ui_layer_nav_set(
    UiState *state,
    ContextId source,
    ContextId target,
    UiPurpose purpose
);

bool ui_layer_nav_try_get(
    const UiState *state,
    ContextId source,
    UiPurpose purpose,
    ContextId *target
);

ContextId ui_layer_nav_get(
    const UiState *state,
    ContextId source,
    UiPurpose purpose
);

bool ui_layer_nav_remove(
    UiState *state,
    ContextId source,
    UiPurpose purpose
);

void ui_layer_nav_clear(
    UiState *state
);

Potentially also:

bool ui_layer_nav_has(
    const UiState *state,
    ContextId source,
    UiPurpose purpose
);

although try_get() often makes that unnecessary.
The resulting mental model

I think you've arrived at a very nice abstraction:

                      UiState
                         │
                         ▼
                  UiNavigation
                         │
                         │
             ┌───────────┴────────────┐
             │                        │
        (ContextId, Purpose)      (ContextId, Purpose)
             │                        │
             ▼                        ▼
         ContextId                ContextId
             │                        │
             └───────────┬────────────┘
                         │
                         ▼
                    ContextLayer

The context layer defines what things are.

The UI layer stores relationships between those things.

The application defines what those relationships mean.

That last part is especially valuable: you're not building a UI framework that dictates "hover means X, selection means Y, navigation means Z." You're providing a sparse, persistent association mechanism over the context graph, which the application can use to construct whatever navigation model it wants.

One small terminology point: I'd probably call your enum UiPurpose rather than ui_purpose, and use values like UI_PURPOSE_HOVER, UI_PURPOSE_SELECTED, etc. The purpose is effectively a namespace/key supplied by the user, not really a bitmask.

1. UiNavigationEntry can absolutely be stored by value

You don't need:

UiNavigationEntry *entry = malloc(...);

for each entry.

Instead, UiNavigation can own one contiguous allocation:

typedef struct UiNavigationEntry {
    ContextId source;
    UiPurpose purpose;
    ContextId target;
} UiNavigationEntry;

typedef struct UiNavigation {
    UiNavigationEntry *entries;

    size_t count;
    size_t capacity;
} UiNavigation;

So if the navigation table grows to 128 entries, you have essentially:

one allocation
       │
       ▼
┌──────────┬──────────┬──────────┬──────────┐
│ Entry    │ Entry    │ Entry    │ Entry    │ ...
└──────────┴──────────┴──────────┴──────────┘

Each UiNavigationEntry is just a few integer values copied around.

No individual allocations.

And yes, the hash table can essentially be the UiNavigation.
2. UiNavigation is the hash table

I'd think of it this way:

typedef struct UiNavigation {
    UiNavigationEntry *entries;
    size_t count;
    size_t capacity;
} UiNavigation;

entries is the hash-table storage.

You don't need:

UiNavigation
    ↓
HashTable
    ↓
UiNavigationEntry*

unless you have a reusable generic hash-table implementation you want to use.

For this specialized case, a dedicated implementation is probably better.

So:

UiState
 ├── current
 ├── selection
 └── navigation
       │
       ├── entries[]
       ├── count
       └── capacity

navigation itself is the hash table.
3. Yes, the hash key consists of two values

Your logical key is:

(source ContextId, UiPurpose)

For example:

(42, HOVER) → 100
(42, RESUME) → 101
(100, HOVER) → 105

The hash function takes both:

uint64_t hash_navigation_key(
    ContextId source,
    UiPurpose purpose
);

Then the table finds the appropriate slot.

But the hash itself is not enough to establish equality.

You do:

if (entry->source == source &&
    entry->purpose == purpose)
{
    // found it
}

This is important because two different keys can have the same hash.

So the process is:

                source + purpose
                       │
                       ▼
                     hash
                       │
                       ▼
                  table slot
                       │
                       ▼
             compare actual values
                /             \
             equal          different
               │                │
             found          probe next

That's standard hash-table behavior.
4. And yes, the target is simply copied

If you do:

ui_layer_nav_set(
    &state,
    source,
    target,
    UI_PURPOSE_RESUME
);

the entry stores:

entry->source = source;
entry->purpose = purpose;
entry->target = target;

All three are values.

No pointers to contexts.

No ownership issue.

So the table might contain:

┌────────┬─────────┬────────┐
│ source │ purpose │ target │
├────────┼─────────┼────────┤
│  100   │ RESUME  │  105   │
│  100   │ HOVER   │  108   │
│  105   │ RESUME  │  112   │
└────────┴─────────┴────────┘

This is exactly the sort of thing that works very nicely in C.
5. Your context events idea fits this architecture very well

I like this approach:

ContextLayer
      │
      │ events
      ▼
UiLayer
      │
      ├── UiState A
      │     └── navigation
      │
      ├── UiState B
      │     └── navigation
      │
      └── UiState C
            └── navigation

Suppose the context layer reports:

Context 100 removed
suggested replacement: 101

The UI layer can update:

(100, HOVER)  → 100
(100, RESUME) → 105
(105, HOVER)  → 100

depending on your policy.
6. But I would make the context event generic

I'd avoid making the context layer say:

    "UI, you should replace this ID with that ID."

The context layer shouldn't know the UI exists.

Instead, something like:

typedef enum ContextEventType {
    CONTEXT_EVENT_REMOVED,
    CONTEXT_EVENT_REPLACED,
    CONTEXT_EVENT_CHANGED,
} ContextEventType;

and:

typedef struct ContextEvent {
    ContextEventType type;

    ContextId id;
    ContextId replacement;
} ContextEvent;

The context layer publishes:

Context 100 was removed.
A reasonable replacement is 101.

The UI layer decides what to do with that.

That keeps the dependency direction correct:

Context layer
      ↓
   events
      ↓
 UI layer

not:

Context layer
      ↓
knows about UI

7. ui_layer_update() is a reasonable API

Your proposed:

ui_layer_update(UiLayer *);

makes sense.

You could have:

void ui_layer_update(UiLayer *ui)
{
    while (context_layer_next_event(
        ui->contexts,
        &event))
    {
        ui_process_context_event(ui, &event);
    }
}

Then the application does:

while (running) {
    context_update(contexts);

    ui_layer_update(&ui);

    application_update(...);

    render(...);
}

This is a nice architecture.
8. But I would avoid scanning every entry on every update

This is the one part I'd reconsider.

You said:

    the UI layer would check all the UI_STATES and the UiNavigationEntry entries if there are ids from the messages.

That is simple and perfectly valid initially.

But consider:

100 UiStates
×
10,000 navigation entries
=
1,000,000 entries scanned per update

If context events are rare, that's unnecessary work.

I'd make ui_layer_update() process only the IDs that appeared in events.

For each removed context:

ContextId 100 removed

you need to find:

source == 100

or:

target == 100

The problem is that your primary hash table is indexed by:

(source, purpose)

so finding entries where target == 100 requires scanning.
9. You have two possible strategies
Simple strategy

Just scan everything:

event
  ↓
all UiStates
  ↓
all navigation entries

This is very easy and might be completely adequate.

I would actually start here unless your numbers are large.
Indexed strategy

Maintain a secondary index:

target ContextId → navigation entries referring to it

Then:

Context 100 removed
       ↓
target index
       ↓
find all references to 100

But now you have two data structures to keep consistent:

(source, purpose) → target

target → references

I'd not introduce this complexity early.
10. There is an even simpler solution with your generation IDs

If your ContextId contains a generation:

typedef struct ContextId {
    uint32_t index;
    uint32_t generation;
} ContextId;

then you may not need to eagerly clean every stale UI navigation entry.

Suppose:

UI:
(100,generation=5, RESUME) → (101,generation=2)

Context 101 gets removed.

The next context using slot 101 becomes:

(101,generation=3)

The old target:

(101,generation=2)

is automatically invalid.

So the UI can lazily discard it when accessed.

That's a very powerful property.

You can still process events to provide replacement suggestions, but stale references aren't dangerous.
11. Your event could contain multiple replacement candidates

Your "suggestions on what IDs to use instead" idea is interesting.

I'd perhaps define the event more like:

typedef struct ContextRemovalEvent {
    ContextId removed;

    const ContextId *suggestions;
    size_t suggestion_count;
} ContextRemovalEvent;

Then the context layer could say:

Removed:
    100

Suggestions:
    101
    102
    103

The UI layer doesn't have to blindly choose one.

The application can decide its own policy.

For example:

Hover:
    choose nearest sibling

Resume:
    choose parent

Selected:
    remove selection

Focus:
    choose first valid child

That is much more flexible.
12. In fact, I wouldn't necessarily let ui_layer_update() automatically choose replacements

This is an important architectural choice.

Suppose:

Context 100 is removed.

and you have:

(A, HOVER) → 100
(B, RESUME) → 100
(C, FOCUS) → 100

There isn't necessarily one correct replacement.

You might want:

HOVER → nearest sibling
RESUME → parent
FOCUS → first child

So I'd make ui_layer_update() responsible for invalidating/processing the event, but perhaps not deciding application semantics.

For example:

void ui_layer_update(UiLayer *ui);

could:

    consume context events;
    invalidate obviously dead IDs;
    optionally notify the user;
    leave policy-specific replacement to user callbacks.

13. A callback-based event mechanism might fit

For example:

typedef void (*UiContextRemovedFn)(
    UiState *state,
    ContextId removed,
    const ContextId *suggestions,
    size_t suggestion_count,
    void *userdata
);

But I'd be cautious about adding callbacks too early.

Your simple model:

ui_layer_update(&ui);

is easier to reason about.

You can later add callbacks if needed.
14. One thing I'd change in UiLayer

If UiLayer contains multiple UiStates, then yes:

struct UiLayer {
    ContextLayer *contexts;

    UiState **states;
    size_t state_count;
    size_t state_capacity;
};

could work.

But I still prefer the possibility that UiState is user-owned.

For example:

typedef struct UiLayer {
    ContextLayer *contexts;

    // Context event processing state.
    ...
} UiLayer;

while:

typedef struct UiState {
    ContextId current;
    UiSelection selection;
    UiNavigation navigation;
} UiState;

is independently created by the user.

Then:

UiState main_view = {0};
UiState sidebar_view = {0};
UiState search_view = {0};

The user can register them with the UI layer if ui_layer_update() needs to process all of them:

ui_layer_add_state(&ui, &main_view);
ui_layer_add_state(&ui, &sidebar_view);
ui_layer_add_state(&ui, &search_view);

Now the ownership is explicit:

Application owns UiState
          │
          ▼
      UiLayer tracks it

rather than:

UiLayer owns UiState

I think this is especially appropriate for a C library.
15. There is a useful distinction between UiLayer and UiState

I'd make this explicit in the design:
UiLayer

Infrastructure shared by views:

- ContextLayer reference
- context event processing
- registered UiStates
- possibly allocator

UiState

A particular view/session:

- current ContextId
- selection
- navigation table

So:

                   UiLayer
                      │
             ContextLayer*
                      │
        ┌─────────────┼─────────────┐
        │             │             │
        ▼             ▼             ▼
     UiState A     UiState B     UiState C
        │             │             │
   navigation     navigation    navigation

That separation is very useful.
16. One subtle API improvement: pass UiLayer separately

Instead of:

ui_layer_nav_set(
    &state,
    ...
);

I'd consider:

ui_layer_nav_set(
    &ui,
    &state,
    source,
    target,
    purpose
);

Why?

Because the UI layer might need the context layer to validate IDs:

context_exists(ui->contexts, source)
context_exists(ui->contexts, target)

It also means all UI operations have access to the same infrastructure.

But there is a tradeoff: if UiNavigation is supposed to be completely independent storage, then requiring UiLayer isn't necessary.

I'd probably separate the two:

ui_nav_set(&state.navigation, source, target, purpose);

for pure storage, and:

ui_layer_update(&ui);

for integration with the context layer.

That's cleaner.
17. So the API might settle into three levels
Pure navigation storage

ui_nav_set(...)
ui_nav_get(...)
ui_nav_remove(...)
ui_nav_clear(...)

These don't know about contexts beyond IDs.
UI state

ui_state_init(...)
ui_state_reset(...)

and direct access to:

state.current
state.selection
state.navigation

UI/context integration

ui_layer_update(...)
ui_layer_add_state(...)
ui_layer_remove_state(...)

This keeps responsibilities nicely separated.
18. One final thought about your event processing

Your idea is fundamentally sound:

context layer
     │
     │ events
     ▼
ui_layer_update()
     │
     ├── UiState A
     │     └── navigation
     ├── UiState B
     │     └── navigation
     └── UiState C
           └── navigation

I'd implement the simple full scan first.

Because your navigation entries are compact:

typedef struct UiNavigationEntry {
    ContextId source;
    UiPurpose purpose;
    ContextId target;
} UiNavigationEntry;

a scan is very cache-friendly:

for (each state)
    for (each navigation entry)
        ...

That's much better than it sounds.

If you have:

10 states × 100 entries

it's trivial.

Even:

50 states × 1,000 entries

is only 50,000 tiny entries.

Don't build a secondary reverse index until the actual scale demands it.

And if you use generation-aware ContextIds, you can make event processing even less critical: invalid references become safely detectable, and ui_layer_update() can focus on applying the context layer's explicit removal/replacement information rather than being responsible for memory safety.

Overall, I think your architecture is converging toward something quite elegant:

ContextLayer
    owns objects + IDs
          │
          │ events
          ▼
      UiLayer
    shared integration
          │
          ├─────────────┐
          ▼             ▼
      UiState A      UiState B
          │             │
          ▼             ▼
    UiNavigation   UiNavigation
          │
          ▼
 (ContextId, UiPurpose) → ContextId

And the UI library never needs to know what UiPurpose means. That's probably the most valuable property of the design you're developing.

UiLayer

It can be very small:

typedef struct UiLayer {
    ContextLayer *contexts;
} UiLayer;

Potentially it could eventually contain other UI-wide infrastructure, but it doesn't need to know about individual views.
UiState

The user owns these:

typedef struct UiState {
    ContextId current;
    UiSelection selection;
    UiNavigation navigation;
} UiState;

So the application can have:

UiState main_view;
UiState sidebar_view;
UiState search_view;

with no relationship to the lifetime of UiLayer.

Then:

UiState *states[] = {
    &main_view,
    &sidebar_view,
    &search_view
};

ui_layer_update(
    &ui,
    states,
    sizeof(states) / sizeof(states[0])
);

The ownership is very clean:

Application
│
├── UiState main_view
├── UiState sidebar_view
├── UiState search_view
│
└── UiLayer
      │
      └── ContextLayer *

UiLayer doesn't own any of the states.
It also makes UiLayer stateless with respect to views

This is a nice property.

You could have:

UiLayer ui = {
    .contexts = context_layer
};

and then use it with completely different sets of states:

UiState states_a[] = {
    main_view,
    sidebar_view
};

UiState states_b[] = {
    temporary_view,
    debug_view,
    inspector_view
};

The UiLayer doesn't care.

In fact, you could even update only one state:

UiState *states[] = { &main_view };

ui_layer_update(&ui, states, 1);

That's useful if, for example, a particular view is temporarily inactive.
I'd make the update API const where possible

The states obviously need to be modified when invalid IDs are repaired, so:

void ui_layer_update(
    UiLayer *ui,
    UiState **states,
    size_t state_count
);

is appropriate if the update can modify them.

But the array itself doesn't need to be modified. You could express that as:

void ui_layer_update(
    UiLayer *ui,
    UiState *const *states,
    size_t state_count
);

This means:

states array:       don't modify
UiState objects:    can modify

It's a subtle distinction, and you don't necessarily need it, but I like it for library APIs.
You could also avoid the array entirely for one state

If you find this common:

UiState *states[] = { &main_view };

ui_layer_update(&ui, states, 1);

you might provide:

void ui_layer_update_state(
    UiLayer *ui,
    UiState *state
);

implemented conceptually as:

void ui_layer_update_state(UiLayer *ui, UiState *state)
{
    ui_layer_update(ui, &state, 1);
}

But I'd only add that if it's actually useful.
One more architectural benefit

This makes UiLayer and UiState have very clear responsibilities:

UiLayer
    ContextLayer *
    context-event integration
    ID validation/replacement
    UI-layer-wide operations

UiState
    current ContextId
    selection
    sparse navigation relationships

And the application owns:

which UiStates exist
which UiStates are active
when UiStates are created/destroyed
which UiStates are passed to update

That's a very good fit for C because there is no hidden ownership or allocation happening behind the scenes.

So yes: I'd remove the states array from UiLayer in this version. It makes UiLayer simpler and gives the application more control.

18. My current preference

Given your goal of keeping this sparse, allocation-conscious, and generic, I'd probably settle on:

typedef struct UiNavigationEntry {
    ContextId source;
    UiPurpose purpose;
    ContextId target;
} UiNavigationEntry;

typedef struct UiNavigation {
    UiNavigationEntry *entries;
    size_t count;
    size_t capacity;
} UiNavigation;

typedef struct UiState {
    ContextId current;
    UiSelection selection;     // for now
    UiNavigation navigation;
} UiState;

and not force selection into navigation yet.

Then make UiSelection itself very lightweight and contiguous:

typedef struct UiSelection {
    ContextId *ids;
    size_t count;
    size_t capacity;
} UiSelection;

That's only one extra allocation for a state that actually has a selection.

And the semantics are extremely clear.

The architectural picture is then:

UiState
│
├── current
│
├── navigation
│     └── sparse hash table
│           (source, purpose) → target
│
└── selection
      └── sparse dynamic array
            [ContextId, ContextId, ...]

Both are ID-only, both are owned by the UI state, neither allocates per context, and both grow only when the user actually uses them.

That is not necessarily an architectural inconsistency. They're two different data structures because they're solving two different cardinality problems.


