EVENTS DETAILS
The core problem

You have something roughly like:

Data layer
    ↓
Context layer
    ├── ContextId → context*
    └── events: ContextId created/removed/changed/...

UI layer
    ├── ui_states
    │     └── ui_navigation
    │           └── entries: (source, purpose, target)
    │
    └── user queries
          ├── get_name(ContextId)
          ├── get_children(ContextId)
          ├── get_parent(ContextId)
          └── ...

Suppose:

101 --LAST_VISITED--> 105

and later the Context layer reports:

CONTEXT_REMOVED(105)

You correctly identify that this can be expensive if the UI layer responds by doing:

for every ui_state
    for every navigation entry
        if entry.target == 105
            ...

every time an event occurs.

That is especially undesirable because the event itself already tells you the identity of what changed.
I would introduce a UI-side ContextId status/index

When the Context layer produces:

CONTEXT_REMOVED(105)

the UI layer should process that event once and record something like:

typedef enum {
    UI_CONTEXT_VALID,
    UI_CONTEXT_REMOVED,
    UI_CONTEXT_UNKNOWN
} UiContextStatus;

Conceptually:

Context event
     │
     ▼
UI event processing
     │
     ├── ContextId 105 → REMOVED
     │
     ▼
UI-side ContextId status/index

Then your UI API can cheaply do:

ui_get_name(ui, 105, &result);

and internally:

is 105 known to be removed?
       │
       ├── no → perform normal operation
       │
       └── yes → return CONTEXT_REMOVED

This is essentially lazy invalidation.

You don't have to immediately walk every navigation entry, every UI state, etc.
Your result-struct idea is particularly good

I like this part of your proposal:

    UI layer returns a result struct with an enum with for example CONTEXT_REMOVED and an array with suggestions

That's a good API boundary.

For example:

typedef enum {
    UI_RESULT_OK,
    UI_RESULT_CONTEXT_REMOVED,
    UI_RESULT_CONTEXT_INVALID,
    UI_RESULT_ERROR
} UiResultCode;

typedef struct {
    ContextId id;
    // perhaps a reason/type/etc.
} UiContextSuggestion;

typedef struct {
    UiResultCode code;

    union {
        struct {
            const char *name;
        } value;

        struct {
            const UiContextSuggestion *items;
            size_t count;
        } invalid_context;
    };
} UiResult;

Though I'd probably avoid making the result struct enormous or putting too many operation-specific fields into one union.

You could instead separate the status from the operation result:

typedef struct {
    UiResultCode code;
    ContextId id;
} UiResult;

and have the caller ask for recovery information separately.

Or:

typedef struct {
    UiResultCode code;

    const ContextId *suggestions;
    size_t suggestion_count;
} UiContextResult;

depending on how central the concept becomes.
One thing I would change: don't necessarily return "suggestions" from get_name()

This is the architectural part I'd be careful about.

Imagine:

ui_get_name(105)

and 105 has been removed.

Should get_name() know that possible replacements are:

101
103
104
107
...

?

Maybe — but this starts mixing context validity with navigation/recovery policy.

I'd lean toward:

ui_get_name(ui, 105, &name)

returning:

UI_CONTEXT_REMOVED

Then:

ui_context_recovery_candidates(ui, 105, &candidates)

could return candidates.

Or even better, the UI state itself can determine appropriate candidates:

ui_resolve_removed_context(ui, state, 105, ...)

because "what should replace this context?" is inherently dependent on UI state and navigation semantics.

For example, if 105 was the current context, the best replacement might be:

    parent of 105
    previous sibling
    next sibling
    last visited context
    root

But another UI state might have a completely different policy.

So I'd distinguish:

Context layer:
    "105 no longer exists."

UI layer:
    "105 is invalid."

UI navigation/application:
    "Given that 105 is invalid, what should I navigate to?"

That separation will probably serve you well.
Even better: process events at controlled points

There's another subtle issue with your proposal.

You said:

    UI layer checks if there are messages from the context layer.

This is reasonable, but I wouldn't literally have every function repeatedly scan the Context-layer message queue.

Instead, have something like:

ui_layer_process_events(ui);

or internally process events at defined synchronization points.

For example:

ui_begin_frame(ui);

/* Context events are consumed here. */

... user/application code ...

ui_end_frame(ui);

Then:

Context layer
       │
       │ events
       ▼
ui_layer_process_events()
       │
       ├── update validity index
       ├── invalidate relevant state
       └── update other UI bookkeeping

After that, normal queries are cheap.

If your program isn't frame-based, the same principle applies: define a clear event synchronization boundary.
What about your navigation entries?

This is where I think your architecture can become particularly nice.

Suppose:

typedef struct {
    ContextId source;
    enum purpose purpose;
    ContextId target;
} ui_navigation_entry;

You have:

101 --LAST_VISITED--> 105

Then 105 disappears.

Don't necessarily delete that navigation entry immediately.

You can leave:

101 --LAST_VISITED--> 105

and mark 105 invalid.

Then when the user asks:

ui_navigation_get(ui, 101, LAST_VISITED)

you get:

target = 105

but when attempting to use 105:

ui_get_name(ui, 105)

you get:

UI_CONTEXT_REMOVED

This is a perfectly valid form of lazy cleanup.

You can subsequently remove or repair the navigation entry if/when appropriate.

This is often preferable to eagerly walking all navigation structures whenever a context disappears.
But you may eventually want reverse indexes

There is one situation where I would maintain an additional index.

Suppose your UI has millions of navigation entries and you eventually need to answer:

    "Which UI objects/navigation relationships reference ContextId 105?"

Don't scan everything.

Maintain a reverse relationship:

ContextId
    ↓
references
    ↓
navigation entries

For example:

ContextId 105
    ├── navigation entry #17
    ├── navigation entry #204
    ├── navigation entry #891
    └── ...

Then:

CONTEXT_REMOVED(105)
        │
        ▼
reverse index[105]
        │
        ├── entry #17
        ├── entry #204
        └── entry #891

Now cleanup is proportional to:

number of references to 105

rather than:

number of all UI states × number of navigation entries

That's a much more scalable architecture.

But don't add this prematurely. If you have 1000 navigation entries, a linear scan may be simpler and completely adequate.
I would also distinguish "invalid" from "removed"

This could become important.

For example:

typedef enum {
    CONTEXT_STATUS_VALID,
    CONTEXT_STATUS_REMOVED,
    CONTEXT_STATUS_UNAVAILABLE
} ContextStatus;

Because eventually you may have cases such as:

Context exists but cannot currently be displayed
Context exists but permissions changed
Context is loading
Context was destroyed
ContextId was never valid

In particular, "removed" is an event; "invalid" is a state.

That's a useful conceptual distinction.

EVENT:
    CONTEXT_REMOVED(105)

STATE:
    105 → REMOVED

The event happens once.

The state can be queried many times.

That's exactly why your proposed architecture can work well.
One important point about ContextId lifetime

You said:

    these ids are unique for the lifetime of the program

That's excellent.

It means you can safely interpret:

105

as a historical identity even after the underlying context disappears.

I would preserve that property.

Don't recycle:

105 → context A
...
context A removed
...
105 → context B   // avoid this

Instead:

105 → context A
...
105 → REMOVED

106 → context B

That makes stale references much easier to reason about.

Your navigation table can therefore safely contain historical IDs without ambiguity.
One architecture I'd consider

I'd structure the responsibility roughly like this:

                  DATA LAYER
                      │
                      ▼
                CONTEXT LAYER
                      │
              ContextId + events
                      │
                      ▼
                 UI LAYER
          ┌───────────┴───────────┐
          │                       │
   Context status/index      UI states
          │                       │
          │                 navigation entries
          │                       │
          └───────────┬───────────┘
                      ▼
              UI/application API

And conceptually:

context_event e;
while (context_poll_event(context, &e)) {
    ui_process_context_event(ui, &e);
}

For:

CONTEXT_REMOVED(105)

the UI layer might do:

ui_context_status[105] = REMOVED

and perhaps update some reverse indexes if you have them.

Then:

ui_get_name(ui, 105, ...)

can immediately return:

UI_CONTEXT_REMOVED

without touching the Context layer.

And finally the application decides:

105 removed
    │
    ▼
find recovery candidate
    │
    ├── parent?
    ├── previous sibling?
    ├── LAST_VISITED?
    └── root?

That last part is application/navigation policy, rather than Context-layer responsibility.
So, overall

Yes, I think your architecture is sound. In fact, the important idea is this:

    Don't propagate context destruction by eagerly repairing every UI reference. Propagate the fact that the ContextId became invalid, and let consumers resolve/recover from the invalid reference when necessary.

I'd make three refinements:

    Convert Context events into UI-side state/index information once, rather than checking the event queue from every API call.
    Return explicit status/result information such as UI_CONTEXT_REMOVED from UI queries.
    Keep recovery/navigation policy separate from context validity. The Context layer says "105 is gone"; the UI/application decides "therefore I'll navigate to 101."

That gives you a nice separation between identity, lifetime, UI state, and navigation policy, and it should scale considerably better than scanning every ui_navigation_entry whenever a context disappears.
If, after implementing it, you find that 90% of your UI state consists of (source, purpose) → N ContextIds, then I'd merge them into a generalized multi-target navigation system. I wouldn't complicate the initial design before you know that's actually the dominant use case.



// Single context, purpose -> multi target relationship
typedef struct {
    ContextId *items;
    size_t count;
    size_t capacity;
} UI_TARGET_LIST;

typedef struct {
    ContextId context;
    UiPurpose purpose;
    UI_TARGET_LIST targets;
    EntryState state;
} UI_NAVIGATION_ENTRY;

typedef struct {
    UI_NAVIGATION_ENTRY *entries;
    size_t count;
    size_t capacity;

    size_t borrow_count;
} UI_NAVIGATION;

Then:

UI_TARGET_LIST *
ui_navigation_get_targets(
    UI_STATE *state,
    ContextId context,
    UiPurpose purpose);

increments:

navigation->borrow_count++;

And:

void
ui_navigation_release_targets(
    UI_STATE *state);

decrements it.
Why a counter rather than a bool?

This is an important detail.

Suppose later you do:

UI_TARGET_LIST *a =
    ui_navigation_get_targets(&state, A, P1);

UI_TARGET_LIST *b =
    ui_navigation_get_targets(&state, B, P2);

Now you have two outstanding borrows.

A boolean doesn't represent that correctly:

borrowed = true

Then releasing a would make it:

borrowed = false

even though b is still being used.

A counter solves this:

borrow_count = 2

release a
    ↓
borrow_count = 1

release b
    ↓
borrow_count = 0

Then the rule is:

if (navigation->borrow_count != 0)
    /* cannot modify hash table */

You could make the API symmetrical

For example:

UI_TARGET_LIST *
ui_navigation_get_targets(
    UI_STATE *state,
    ContextId context,
    UiPurpose purpose);

void
ui_navigation_release_targets(
    UI_STATE *state);

Usage:

UI_TARGET_LIST *targets =
    ui_navigation_get_targets(&state, context, purpose);

if (targets != NULL) {
    ui_target_list_add(targets, id1);
    ui_target_list_add(targets, id2);
    ui_target_list_add(targets, id3);

    ui_navigation_release_targets(&state);
}

The important contract would be:

    A UI_TARGET_LIST * returned by ui_navigation_get_targets() is valid until ui_navigation_release_targets().

And:

    While a target list is borrowed, operations that can modify the navigation hash table are prohibited.

But there's a subtle problem with get_targets()

Suppose the entry doesn't exist.

You call:

ui_navigation_get_targets(&state, A, P);

The function needs to create the hash-table entry.

But creating it might require a rehash.

So the sequence needs to be:

get_targets()
    │
    ├── find entry
    │
    ├── if absent:
    │      maybe resize hash table
    │      create entry
    │
    └── THEN increment borrow_count

You must not increment the borrow count before the lookup/insertion is complete.

Otherwise you'd have:

borrow_count > 0

while simultaneously needing to rehash.

So internally:

UI_TARGET_LIST *
ui_navigation_get_targets(...)
{
    UI_NAVIGATION_ENTRY *entry;

    /* No borrow exists yet. */
    entry = find_or_create_entry(...);

    if (entry == NULL)
        return NULL;

    navigation->borrow_count++;

    return &entry->targets;
}

That's clean.
What operations should be blocked?

I'd distinguish between entry operations and target-list operations.

While borrowed, this should be forbidden:

ui_navigation_insert(...)
ui_navigation_remove(...)
ui_navigation_clear(...)
ui_navigation_rehash(...)

because these can change:

navigation->entries

But this should remain perfectly valid:

ui_target_list_add(targets, id);
ui_target_list_remove(targets, id);
ui_target_list_clear(targets);

because they only manipulate:

targets->items

and don't move the hash table entries.

So:

BORROWED
│
├── target list operations      OK
│     ├── add
│     ├── remove
│     ├── clear
│     └── resize items[]
│
└── navigation operations       NOT OK
      ├── insert entry
      ├── delete entry
      ├── rehash
      └── resize entries[]

This is a very clean distinction.
One thing I'd be careful about: error handling

Because C doesn't enforce the borrow protocol, the caller can do:

UI_TARGET_LIST *targets =
    ui_navigation_get_targets(...);

/* forgot release */

Now your UI navigation is permanently locked.

That's the major disadvantage of this design.

It's essentially manually managed resource lifetime:

get()
...
...
release()

similar to:

lock();
...
unlock();

or:

malloc();
...
free();

The compiler won't help you.

You therefore want the API/documentation to make the rule extremely obvious.
An even nicer API might be begin/end

I'd actually consider:

UI_TARGET_LIST *
ui_navigation_targets_begin(
    UI_STATE *state,
    ContextId context,
    UiPurpose purpose);

void
ui_navigation_targets_end(
    UI_STATE *state);

The names communicate the lifetime:

UI_TARGET_LIST *targets =
    ui_navigation_targets_begin(&state, context, purpose);

/* use targets */

ui_navigation_targets_end(&state);

Or:

ui_navigation_borrow_targets(...)
ui_navigation_return_targets(...)

The word borrow is particularly good because it communicates that the caller doesn't own the returned object.
Could the borrow state live in UI_TARGET_LIST?

You proposed:

typedef struct {
    ContextId *items;
    size_t count;
    size_t capacity;

    bool borrowed;
} UI_TARGET_LIST;

I wouldn't do that because it gives the wrong abstraction.

Consider:

UI_NAVIGATION
    entries[]
       │
       ├── targets A (borrowed)
       ├── targets B
       └── targets C

The hash table is frozen because A is borrowed.

That has nothing to do with B or C.

So this:

navigation->borrow_count

expresses the actual invariant much better than:

targetsA->borrowed

There is one more improvement I'd strongly consider

Instead of:

size_t borrow_count;

you could have:

size_t borrow_count;

and debug assertions around every operation that can invalidate entry pointers.

For example:

static bool ui_navigation_can_modify(
    const UI_NAVIGATION *navigation)
{
    return navigation->borrow_count == 0;
}

Then:

bool ui_navigation_remove(...)
{
    assert(navigation->borrow_count == 0);

    ...
}

During development, accidentally doing:

UI_TARGET_LIST *targets =
    ui_navigation_get_targets(...);

ui_navigation_remove(...);

would immediately expose the bug.

In release builds you can decide whether to return an error instead.
One concern: UI_STATE copying

Your design also means you need to think about:

UI_STATE a;
UI_STATE b = a;

You presumably already can't safely shallow-copy UI_STATE because it contains dynamically allocated hash-table storage.

But with borrowing, this becomes even more important.

I'd make UI_STATE explicitly non-copyable by convention and provide:

bool ui_state_init(UI_STATE *);
void ui_state_destroy(UI_STATE *);

bool ui_state_clone(UI_STATE *dst, const UI_STATE *src);

And probably:

    ui_state_clone() requires that src has no outstanding borrows.

That's a natural invariant.
Overall, I like this design

Given your architecture, I'd actually prefer this over allocating UI_TARGET_LIST separately.

You get:

                     UI_STATE
                        │
                        ▼
                  UI_NAVIGATION
                        │
             ┌──────────┴──────────┐
             │                     │
        hash entries           borrow_count
             │
             ▼
      UI_NAVIGATION_ENTRY
             │
             ▼
       UI_TARGET_LIST
             │
             ▼
        ContextId[]

The allocations are:

UI_NAVIGATION.entries
        ↓
   hash table storage

UI_TARGET_LIST.items
        ↓
   target collection storage

Only those two things need dynamic allocation.

And your API becomes quite elegant:

UI_TARGET_LIST *
ui_navigation_targets_begin(
    UI_STATE *state,
    ContextId context,
    UiPurpose purpose);

void
ui_navigation_targets_end(UI_STATE *state);

Usage:

UI_TARGET_LIST *targets =
    ui_navigation_targets_begin(&state, context, purpose);

if (targets != NULL) {
    ui_target_list_add(targets, id1);
    ui_target_list_add(targets, id2);
    ui_target_list_add(targets, id3);

    ui_navigation_targets_end(&state);
}

The hash table remains stable for the duration of the borrow, while targets->items is free to realloc().
Allowed while borrowed

ui_target_list_add()
ui_target_list_remove()
ui_target_list_clear()

because they only modify:

ContextId[]

Forbidden while borrowed

ui_navigation_insert()
ui_navigation_remove()
ui_navigation_clear()
ui_navigation_reserve()

because they can modify:

UI_NAVIGATION_ENTRY[]
